{"chunk_id": "c32ba6ac87f7", "text": "This section recounts the rapid downfall of King Nala under the malignant influence of Kali. Obsessed with gambling, Nala steadily loses his wealth and ignores pleas from his citizens and from his wife Damayanti. Realizing the danger, Damayanti secretly acts to protect their children, sending them away with the loyal charioteer Varshneya to her father\u2019s kingdom in Vidarbha. Eventually Pushkara wins Nala\u2019s entire kingdom and cruelly proposes staking Damayanti herself. Refusing to continue, Nala abandons all ornaments and leaves the city with Damayanti, both dressed in a single cloth. Banished and shunned by their own people, they wander in hunger. In a final humiliation, golden birds\u2014revealed to be the very dice that ruined him\u2014steal Nala\u2019s last garment, symbolizing the total stripping away of his fortune and dignity.\nAs Nala loses everything to dice, Damayanti secretly sends their children away for safety before Nala is stripped of kingdom, wealth, and dignity.\nThemes: gambling downfall, loyalty in crisis, protecting children, exile, fate and misfortune", "vector": [0.00537331635132432, -0.023008029907941818, 0.011886117048561573, -0.023813173174858093, -0.023117201402783394, 0.01997850276529789, -0.03321562334895134, -0.0035787993110716343, -0.0285212229937315, -0.023431072011590004, 0.016430407762527466, 0.022189239040017128, 0.023594830185174942, 0.025764625519514084, 0.003068760735914111, 0.013987680897116661, 0.030513614416122437, -0.023212727159261703, 0.027061045169830322, -0.020947406068444252, -0.014724592678248882, 0.002688364125788212, -0.006604914553463459, -0.0008430989691987634, -0.01572078838944435, 0.005444960668683052, 0.008106031455099583, -0.015707142651081085, 0.015120342373847961, -0.005850944668054581, 0.0032700467854738235, -0.0030397619120776653, 0.014301550574600697, -0.02691093273460865, -0.0364907905459404, -0.0038176134694367647, 0.0035480947699397802, -0.005663305055350065,